---
title: 'Extra-Solar-System objects'
---
### The Central Stars

The exoplanets in this Science Olympiad focus on 3 stars all of about the same age:


| Name           | Central Star                | Stellar Type | Distance | V     | K      | Age     | 
|:---------------|:----------------------------|:-------------|:---------|:------|:-------|:--------|
| TRAPPIST-1     | 2MASS J23062928-0502285     | M8 V         | 12 pc    | 18.8  | 10.30  | 7.6 Gyr |
| LHS 1140       | 2MASS J00445930-1516166     | M4.5 V       | 15 pc    | 14.2  | 8.82   | >5 Gyr  |
| Kepler-452     | 2MASS J19440088+4416392     | G2 IV        | 554 pc   | 13.4  | 11.85  | 6 Gyr   |

Alternative names for the central stars are TRAPPIST-1a, LHS 1140a, and Kepler-452a, respectively.

### The Exoplanets

The orbital period of all of the exoplanets around the selected stars were measured
by the **transit photometry** method.  This method is applied most easily to small stars.  

| Name           | Date of discovery  | Discovery Method   | Distance (AU) | Radius | T (days) | Habitable Zone? |
|:---------------|:-------------------|:-------------------|:--------------|:-------|:---------|:----------------|
| TRAPPIST-1 b   | 5/2/2016           | Transit photometry | 0.01154       |        |          |                 |
| TRAPPIST-1 c   |                    | Transit photometry | 0.0158        |        |          |                 |
| TRAPPIST-1 d   |                    | Transit photometry | 0.0223        |        |          | Close           |
| TRAPPIST-1 e   |                    | Transit photometry | 0.02925*      |        |          | Yes             |
| TRAPPIST-1 f   |                    | Transit photometry | 0.03849*      |        |          | Yes             |
| TRAPPIST-1 g   |                    | Transit photometry | 0.04683*      |        |          | Yes                 
| TRAPPIST-1 h   | 2/22/2017          | Transit photometry |               |        |          |                 |
| LHS 1140 b     | 4/20/2017          | Transit photometry |               |        |          |                 |
| LHS 1140 c     | announced in 2018  | Transit photometry |               |        |          |                 |
| Kepler-452 b   | 7/23/2015          | Transit photometry | 1.046         |        | 385      | Yes             | 

Exoplanets in the "habitable zone" meaning their disance from the central star could theoretically allow liquid water to pool.

#### Why focus on low-mass stars?

Compared to massive stars:

* Low-mass stars are more common.  So the neighborhood of the Sun is
more likely to be crowded with low-mass stars than high-mass stars.

* Low-mass stars are smaller. So when a planet-sized objects eclipses a 
low-mass star, it hides a larger fraction of its surface.

* Low-mass stars have lower mass, so planet-sized objects will cause a relatively
larger perturbation in the star's radial velocity.

### REFERENCES:

* [NASA's Exoplanet archive](https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu) 

* [NASA's Table of all Currently Known Exoplanets](https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/cgi-bin/TblView/nph-tblView?app=ExoTbls&config=PS)
Note this table is both very long and very wide.  Scroll right to see all the columns.  Be sure to use
the search bar at the top of each column to find the objects of interest.

* The [Exoplanet Follow-up Observing Program](https://exofop.ipac.caltech.edu/tess/), a repository
for studies of exoplanet candidates.

* An [Exoplanet Science Strategy](https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/25187/chapter/1) 
from the National Academy of Sciences, for finding exoplanets and evaluating their habitability.



